  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4z{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $z$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ z & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2(4y)(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})+2(4y)y+2y(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,600}{y}+8{y}^{2}+\\frac{400}{y} \\\\  & =8{y}^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3}-2\\,000 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2\\,000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4\\,000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{5}=600.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
